CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is an isometric schematic of a deep network drawn as a tall rectangular box standing on end between two labeled faces. The word Data sits below the bottom face and the word Embedding sits above the top face. Inside, three light gray parallelogram planes are stacked at even heights to stand for intermediate layers, with the box's outer edges in solid black and the interior planes outlined in a lighter gray. Five dotted curves thread up through the stack, each starting at a filled round dot on or near the lower planes and ending in an arrowhead just short of the top face, so a single datapoint can be traced through every layer. A long vertical arrow at the far left points upward beside the rotated text Representation learning, and a matching arrow at the far right points downward beside Generative modeling. The two opposed arrows carry the encoding: the same stack of layers is read bottom-up in one case and top-down in the other. The picture sets up the claim that representation learning and generative modeling are two readings of one architecture, and invites the question of what actually separates data from an embedding.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - tall transparent rectangular network volume with black outer wireframe edges
  - top parallelogram face of the box outlined in thick black
  - bottom parallelogram face of the box outlined in thick black
  - four vertical black edges connecting top and bottom faces
  - three evenly spaced internal horizontal layer planes drawn as light gray translucent parallelograms
  - gray outlines around each internal layer plane
  - five dotted curved trajectories threading through the stack from lower data locations toward upper embedding locations
  - five filled circular data-point dots near the lower part of the stack
  - five arrowheads at the upper ends of the dotted trajectories in representation-learning view
  - left-side vertical arrow pointing upward
  - right-side vertical arrow pointing downward
  - large top label: Embedding
  - large bottom label: Data
  - rotated left-side label: Representation learning
  - rotated right-side label: Generative modeling

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Use an axonometric/isometric schematic matching the source: a tall upright rectangular prism, centered. World dimensions: box width X = 4.8 units, height Y = 7.0 units, depth Z = 3.2 units. +X is screen-right, +Y is screen-up, +Z is the receding diagonal. Bottom face is the X-Z rectangle at Y=0; top face is the parallel X-Z rectangle at Y=7. Internal layer planes are X-Z rectangles at Y=1.6, 3.2, and 4.8, each the same size as the box interior, drawn as translucent light gray parallelograms. Outer wireframe edges are black #000000, thick, about 0.04 world units. Internal plane outlines are medium gray #808080 to #9a9a9a, about half the outer edge thickness; plane fill is #eeeeee with opacity about 0.35. Dotted paths should sit inside the prism and curve slightly rather than being perfectly straight. Approximate path anchors at each layer: path A starts near x=-1.8,z=-0.8,y=0.25 and ends near x=-1.5,z=0.4,y=5.5; path B starts x=-0.9,z=-1.0,y=0.2 and ends x=-1.0,z=0.7,y=5.8; path C starts x=0.0,z=-1.1,y=0.2 and ends x=-0.4,z=0.5,y=5.6; path D starts x=0.8,z=-0.8,y=0.2 and ends x=1.0,z=0.4,y=5.7; path E starts x=1.7,z=-0.6,y=0.25 and ends x=1.5,z=0.7,y=5.4. Dots at lower endpoints are filled circles/spheres, mostly black #000000 with a couple medium gray #666666 as in the source. Dotted curves are dark gray #666666, with active direction rendered black or darker. In representation-learning mode, arrowheads are near the upper ends of the paths and point upward toward the top face; in generative-modeling mode, arrowheads flip to the lower ends and point downward toward the bottom face. The left side arrow is outside the box, about x=-4.4, vertical along Y, pointing up; the right side arrow is outside the box, about x=4.4, vertical along Y, pointing down. Labels: 'Embedding' centered above the top face, 'Data' centered below the bottom face, large black serif text. Side labels are vertical/rotated: 'Representation learning' on the left and 'Generative modeling' on the right.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "activeDirection",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Active direction",
    "options": [
      "Representation learning",
      "Generative modeling"
    ],
    "default": "Representation learning",
    "teaches": "The same layered architecture can be read bottom-up as an encoder from data to embedding, or top-down as a generator from embedding to data"
  },
  {
    "id": "layerTraversal",
    "type": "state",
    "label": "Layer traversal",
    "states": [
      "Data",
      "Layer 1",
      "Layer 2",
      "Layer 3",
      "Embedding"
    ],
    "default": "Data",
    "teaches": "A point changes representation gradually as it passes through successive intermediate layers rather than jumping directly from data to embedding"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Read upward: representation learning",
    "narration": "Start at the Data face. The dotted paths and arrowheads move upward through the same stack, showing data being transformed into an embedding.",
    "state": {
      "activeDirection": "Representation learning",
      "layerTraversal": "Data"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Intermediate representation",
    "narration": "Advance to a middle plane. The selected slice highlights the current representation, and the points on that plane show the partially transformed data.",
